{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 35, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Oregon's pioneering effort to decriminalize drug possession is set to end this Sunday, replaced by legislation that reinstates criminal penalties for holding small quantities of hard substances. Under the new statute, individuals found with drugs like fentanyl, heroin, or methamphetamine face a choice between prosecution or entering a treatment protocol. This approach seeks to balance enforcement with health interventions, though personal use remains a misdemeanor carrying potential jail time.\n\n***\n\nThe revised law intends to empower law enforcement to address public drug consumption more aggressively while imposing stricter sanctions on sales near sensitive areas like parks. Although it urges local jurisdictions to establish diversion programs linking people to mental health and addiction services, it does not compel them to do so. Supporters argue this flexibility allows regions to tailor solutions to their specific resources, whereas critics fear it will result in an inconsistent and unfair system across the state.\n\n***\n\nPolice officials note that eligibility for deflection is highly restricted, requiring individuals to have no outstanding warrants, additional charges, or history of violence, and to be medically stable. When criteria are met, officers contact a dispatch line to connect the individual with a mobile health team, which must arrive within thirty minutes. If these professionals cannot appear in that narrow window, the individual will be taken into custody, as officers cannot delay proceedings indefinitely.\n\n***\n\nWhile the situation in this part of the **country** evolves, one might recall how a child's **age** determines their ability to **eat** certain foods or go **shopping** alone. Just as a burnt **tongue** reminds us to be careful with hot meals, these legal shifts serve as a stark reminder of the complexities involved in managing substance abuse policies. The outcome of this experiment will likely influence future debates nationwide.\n\nP.P.S This summary reflects the transition from decriminalization back to a penalty-based framework in Oregon.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -2.384186e-07}, {"token": "'s", "logprob": -0.0047032996}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.019498203}, {"token": " effort", "logprob": -0.41343585}, {"token": " to", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -0.15509635}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.95432556}, {"token": " set", "logprob": -0.019492976}, {"token": " to", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " end", "logprob": -4.5419773e-05}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -2.2173173e-05}, {"token": " Sunday", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -1.3113031e-06}, {"token": " replaced", "logprob": -4.613506e-05}, {"token": " by", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " legislation", "logprob": -1.8119976e-05}, {"token": " that", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " reinst", "logprob": -0.011388894}, {"token": "ates", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " criminal", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " for", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " holding", "logprob": -2.7418175e-06}, {"token": " small", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " quantities", "logprob": -5.3644326e-06}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " hard", "logprob": -0.02340011}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.00018938188}, {"token": " Under", "logprob": -0.00018938188}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " new", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -4.744642e-05}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.009693906}, {"token": " individuals", "logprob": -0.8879699}, {"token": " found", "lo